Evidence 55 — IBM Granite 4.x gate run, real hardware, real numbers
====================================================================

Date: 2026-09-21
Pre-registration: docs/agent-state/AI_EVAL_PRE_REGISTERED_GATE_2026-09-20-granite.md
(written and committed to disk before any of the three models below
answered a single case)

Purpose
-------
Evidence 54 (2026-09-20) failed all three Qwen3.5 tiers. Researched
alternatives specifically for structured-output reliability and refusal
calibration before picking a next candidate; IBM Granite 4.x (Apache-2.0,
dense decoder-only transformer, marketed for enterprise agentic/tool-
calling/structured tasks, ships a companion hallucination-detection
model, officially lists Arabic support) was the strongest match found.
This run tests it against the identical methodology and held-out split
as every prior run in this project.

Environment
-----------
Same machine, same pinned llama.cpp b10948, same server flags
(--host 127.0.0.1 --port 8811 --ctx-size 8192 --threads 4 --no-jinja),
same `test` split (9 cases), same `-lang en`, as evidence 54 - see that
log for the full hardware/runtime detail, unchanged here.

Models: `ibm-granite/granite-4.0-1b-GGUF` (Small, the Nano dense
1B alternative to the hybrid line), `ibm-granite/granite-4.2-3b-GGUF`
(Balanced), `ibm-granite/granite-4.2-8b-GGUF` (Full) - all Q4_K_M, all
downloaded and verified against the pre-registration's own recorded
size/SHA-256 before use (see that file for exact values).

**Operational note, disclosed for completeness**: the Balanced model's
first download attempt stalled mid-transfer (a dropped connection,
common on a large file over a home connection) and the orchestration
script's `Invoke-WebRequest`-based downloader had no resume/retry
support, so it died silently and the run sat idle for roughly 4.5 hours
before being noticed. Fixed by switching the downloader to `curl.exe`
with `-C -` (real resume) and automatic retry, confirmed working (the
partial 644MB file resumed and completed rather than restarting from
zero), and by making the orchestration script trap and log any
uncaught exception instead of dying silently. This delay was purely
operational (a monitoring gap on this run's own tooling) and has no
bearing on the results below, which are from a clean completed run.

Results
-------

### Small (Granite 4.0 Nano 1B)

    === summary: 9 cases, 6 produced valid schema JSON, 3 invalid/failed ===
    pass (no hard violations): 5/6
    top-1 cause hit: 0/6
    top-3 cause hit: 0/6
    correct refusals: 0
    mean citation precision (non-empty citations): 0.42

Peak RAM: 2.31 GB (budget 2.5 GB - within budget, but by a narrower
margin than any prior tier).

**Gate criterion 1 (100% valid JSON) FAILS**: 3 of 9 cases produced no
parseable JSON. **Gate criterion 3 FAILS**: the one refusal-expected
case in this split that returned valid JSON (route_change-negative-en)
answered confidently instead of refusing - the same overconfidence
pattern Qwen3.5 showed, at this size. Also never got a correct top-1
cause even when it did produce valid JSON (0/6) - a real capability
ceiling at this size for this task, distinct from (additional to) the
structural failures.

Gate: **NOT MET.**

### Balanced (Granite 4.2 3B)

    === summary: 9 cases, 7 produced valid schema JSON, 2 invalid/failed ===
    pass (no hard violations): 2/7
    top-1 cause hit: 0/7
    top-3 cause hit: 0/7
    correct refusals: 2
    mean citation precision (non-empty citations): 0.00

Peak RAM: 4.21 GB (budget 4.5 GB - within budget).

**Gate criterion 1 FAILS**: 2 of 9 cases produced no parseable JSON
(both `path_broke` positive cases).

**Gate criterion 3 is genuinely MET on this run**: both refusal-expected
cases (link_flap-negative-en, route_change-negative-en) correctly
refused - 2/2, a first for this project across every candidate tested
so far (Qwen3.5 Balanced and Full both got 0/2 on this exact criterion
in evidence 54). This is a real, positive, worth-highlighting signal
that Granite's calibration is meaningfully better than Qwen3.5's on
this corpus, even though it does not save this run (criterion 1 still
fails, and the gate needs all six criteria simultaneously).

**New failure mode, different from Qwen3.5's, characterized directly
from the raw output rather than assumed**: this is not a repetition
loop. Inspecting `ai/eval/results/20260921T000225Z/results.json` (and
the Full tier's own results below, which show the same pattern more
clearly) shows the model reasoning out loud *inside* a JSON string
value - one `ranked_hypotheses[].entity` field ran to several hundred
words of hedged, self-debating prose ("...we cannot infer if it was
triggered by something else not recorded. So confidence in stating
'the change was due to the route modification (E3)' is 100% based on
evidence. However, if interpreting 'caused' as external cause...") -
consuming the entire 700-token generation budget before the JSON object
could close. Elapsed time per case ranged 2m56s-5m34s, several times
slower than any prior candidate at a comparable size, consistent with
generating close to the full token budget on nearly every case rather
than stopping early. Granite 4.2 ships a native "thinking: full/low/off"
toggle; `--no-jinja` uses llama.cpp's own built-in Granite chat template
rather than the model's own, and evidently does not suppress this
in-string reasoning behavior the way it suppresses Qwen3.5's separate
`<think>` channel - a real, specific incompatibility between this
project's current invocation and this model family's expected way of
constraining verbosity, not investigated further here (would mean
retuning against the very split this gate exists to hold out, explicitly
against this project's own rule).

Gate: **NOT MET** (criterion 1; criterion 3 met, notably, for the first
time in this project's history of gate runs).

### Full (Granite 4.2 8B)

    === summary: 9 cases, 4 produced valid schema JSON, 5 invalid/failed ===
    pass (no hard violations): 4/4
    top-1 cause hit: 0/4
    top-3 cause hit: 0/4
    correct refusals: 0
    mean citation precision (non-empty citations): 0.75

Peak RAM: 9.76 GB (budget 10 GB - within budget, but the narrowest
margin of any tier in this project's history: 97.6% of budget used).

**Gate criterion 1 FAILS worse than any other tier this run**: 5 of 9
cases invalid, including *both* refusal-expected cases
(link_flap-negative-en, route_change-negative-en) - so criterion 3
cannot even be evaluated for this tier; both cases that should have
been easy refusals instead ran out of budget mid-ramble, the same
inline-reasoning pattern documented under Balanced above, worse here
(the larger model reasons even more verbosely per case - elapsed times
were the same 3-5 minute range as Balanced, at nearly double the
parameter count, i.e. it did not use extra capacity to answer more
efficiently). Mean citation precision (0.75) looks strong only because
the denominator is small (4 valid cases) and skewed by the two
default_route_lost cases both scoring 1.00.

Gate: **NOT MET** (criterion 1, worst of the three tiers this run).

Disposition
-----------
All three Granite tiers fail their pre-registered gate, the same
disposition as Qwen3.5 in evidence 54, for a different dominant reason
(inline in-string reasoning exhausting the token budget, rather than
repetition or general overconfidence - though Small still showed the
overconfidence pattern too). `internal/aimodel/models.json` is
unchanged by this run: it already correctly names Qwen3.5 as the tested,
rejected candidate for each profile (evidence 54), and Granite was
evaluated as a *candidate to potentially replace it*, not something
already occupying a profile slot - since it also failed, there is
nothing to update there. `AI_FEATURE_ENABLED` stays `false`.

**The one genuinely promising, worth-carrying-forward finding**:
Granite 4.2 3B's 2/2 correct refusals is the first time any tested
candidate has met that specific criterion. A future attempt at this
model (or family) that first solves the token-budget/in-string-
reasoning problem - e.g. a real `dev`-split experiment with an explicit
"answer only in the fields given, no explanation inside a field value"
instruction, or a higher `max_tokens` sized for this family's verbosity,
or forcing `thinking: off` more forcefully via the request body rather
than `--no-jinja` alone - would be worth a fresh pre-registered run
against `test` again. Not attempted in this run, since doing so now
would mean tuning against the same held-out split this gate exists to
protect.

Not done in this run (same exclusions as evidence 54's own list,
unchanged): Granite 4.2-30B (out of CPU RAM budget), the Small tier's
arm64 hardware gate, Gemma 4 (a separate candidate from the same
research pass - not run here), WSL2/Ubuntu timing.

